course: medicine
lesson: 42
title: Swimming and cycling
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Swimming is the one sport that can save your life. There are four strokes, front crawl, backstroke, breaststroke and butterfly, and in England children are meant to swim at least 25 metres by the end of primary school.
explain: Water has its own rules: real drowning is often silent, with the head low and no waving, so if someone looks wrong, shout for the lifeguard at once. Never jump in after a person or a dog: call, reach or throw from the bank.
explain: Fall into cold water and your body gasps for about a minute, so the RNLI says Float to Live: lean back, spread your arms and legs, and float until your breathing calms, then call for help.
explain: On a bike, a level, buckled helmet and brakes that work matter more than looking cool. Picture the ABC check on the drive, Air, Brakes, Chain, then Bikeability training before you ride on the road.
fact: The four swimming strokes are front crawl, backstroke, breaststroke and butterfly; in front crawl the arms do most of the pushing and the legs kick mainly to keep the body flat.
fact: In England the school curriculum says children should be able to swim at least 25 metres by the end of primary school.
fact: Cold water makes you gasp; getting in slowly lets the body settle, and open water is colder and deeper than a pool and has currents, so swim outdoors only where there are lifeguards.
fact: There is no good evidence that swimming after a normal meal makes you drown; after a huge meal a short rest is just for comfort.
fact: Stinging eyes and the strong pool smell come mostly from chloramines, made when chlorine mixes with sweat and wee, so shower and use the toilet before you swim.
fact: A person who is drowning usually can't shout or wave and can look calm, with the mouth at the water line; tell a lifeguard straight away.
fact: People who jump in to rescue someone, or a dog, often get into trouble themselves; call 999 (or 112) and reach or throw something from the bank, and never go in.
fact: Cold shock, gasping and fast breathing, lasts about the first minute or two; the RNLI advice is Float to Live: lean back, spread your arms and legs, and float until breathing calms.
fact: A bike helmet's foam crushes in a crash so the brain is shaken less; it should sit level, about two fingers above the eyebrows, buckled snugly, and be replaced after a big crash.
fact: In the UK the Highway Code advises cyclists to wear a helmet.
fact: Worn brake pads or loose cables mean you can't stop in time; the ABC check is Air in the tyres, Brakes and Chain.
fact: Bikeability is UK cycle training: Level 1 teaches control away from traffic, Level 2 riding on quiet roads, Level 3 busier roads.
fact: The big thigh muscles push the pedals down, and you stay balanced mostly through tiny steering movements; moving forward makes balancing easier, and gears help on hills.
exercise: Chloe from Brisbane grabs her togs (swimsuit). Name the four strokes. | Front crawl, backstroke, breaststroke, butterfly | Front crawl, doggy paddle, diving and floating on your back | Butterfly, sidestroke, treading, sculling | Backstroke, breaststroke, diving, paddling
exercise: Chloe swims one length of each of the four strokes in a 25 m pool. How far altogether? | 100 m | 25 m | 75 m | 50 m
exercise: In front crawl, what gives most of the push? | The arms | The legs, kicking hard | The head, steering the way | The breathing, which lifts the body
exercise: How far should children in England be able to swim by the end of primary school? | At least 25 metres | At least 1 metre without help | At least 5 kilometres, in open water | At least 400 metres
exercise: Ryan from Glasgow is scared of the freezing pool. What helps? | Getting in slowly so the body settles | Jumping straight in head first to get it over with | Holding his breath for a whole minute first | Swimming in the sea instead, where it's warmer
exercise: Why is the sea harder than a pool? | It's colder and deeper and has currents | It's always warmer than a pool, so you tire faster | The salt in it makes you sink faster | The waves always push you back to the beach
exercise: Grace's nan: "Wait an hour after a sandwich or you'll drown!" What's true? | There's no good evidence for that rule | Yes, you must wait exactly an hour or you'll get cramp | Only bread is dangerous to eat before swimming | You must wait three hours
exercise: Ethan's eyes sting. What mostly causes the strong pool smell? | Chlorine mixing with sweat and wee | The pool being extra clean and fresh | Too little chlorine in the water for its size | Salt added to the water to soften it
exercise: Isla sees a boy bobbing quietly, mouth at the water line, not waving. What should she do? | Shout for the lifeguard at once | Wait until he waves or shouts for help | Jump in and grab him herself, quickly | Ignore it, drowning is always loud and noisy
exercise: Sean's dog falls into the canal in Cork. What's safe? | Call for help and reach or throw from the bank | Jump straight in after the dog | Wade in up to his chest | Climb down and hold the dog's collar in the water
exercise: Amelia falls off her paddleboard into cold sea. What should she do first? | Lean back and float until her breathing calms | Swim flat out for the shore before she gets cold | Climb onto the nearest rock wall at once | Dive under the water to get warm
exercise: About how long does cold shock last? | About the first minute or two | About a whole day | About one second | About an hour
exercise: Leo's helmet "messes his hair". How should a helmet sit? | Level, two fingers above the eyebrows, buckled | Pushed back on the head to show the forehead | Unbuckled, so it can slip off easily in a fall | Tilted down over the eyes to shade them
exercise: Maya's bike squeals and takes ages to stop. What's the ABC check? | Air, Brakes, Chain | Arms, Back, Chin | Air, Bell, Colour | Axle, Bars, Comfort
exercise: Arjun does Bikeability. What does Level 2 teach? | Riding on quiet roads | Riding on motorways and dual carriageways | Racing round a velodrome track | Doing wheelies and jumps safely
exercise: Jack from Winnipeg rides home in his toque (woolly hat), legs burning. Which muscles push the pedals most? | The big front thigh muscles | The arm muscles pulling on the bars | The tummy muscles only | The neck muscles
exercise: What mostly keeps a moving bike upright? | Tiny steering movements by the rider | The spinning wheels on their own, like a top | The heavy weight of the metal frame | The air pushing on both sides
test: Ryan: "I'm no goin' in, I'm nesh (soft about cold)." What would happen if he jumped straight in? | He'd likely gasp; getting in slowly is better | Nothing, cold water has no effect on the body at all | He'd warm up faster than if he eased in slowly | He'd float better in the cold
test: "Wait an hour or ye'll get cramp and drown, so ye will!" Grace has had a sandwich. What can she do? | Swim, after a snack there's no need to wait | Wait at least an hour before going in | Never swim on the same day as eating bread | Only swim on her back
test: Ethan: "This chlorine is sus, the pool's too clean." What's the truth? | A strong smell often means sweat and wee are reacting | The smell means the pool is perfectly clean and safe | Chlorine only smells when the pool is nearly empty | Stinging eyes mean too little water
test: In films drowning people scream and splash. What is real drowning often like? | Quiet, head low, no waving | Loud screaming and splashing for minutes | Waving both arms high above the head | Calling out names clearly
test: Sean's mate: "Ah sure, I'll jump in after him, it'll be grand." What's the rule? | Call, reach or throw, never go in | Jump in, brave rescuers are always fine | Jump in if the water looks calm and shallow | Go in if you can swim 25 m
test: Amelia's thrashing towards the beach, gasping. What would happen if she kept that up? | She could breathe in water and tire | She'd reach the beach faster and safer | Thrashing about warms the body up safely | Nothing, panic helps in cold water
test: Leo: "Helmet's giving dork, bro." What's the fact? | The foam crushes in a crash so the brain is shaken less | Helmets only protect your hair and ears | Helmets work best tipped back on the head | Helmets make crashes more likely
test: Maya: "It still rides, so it's fine, innit?" Brakes squeal and are slow. What's true? | A bike that can't stop in time isn't safe | If it rides, it's safe enough | Squeaky brakes stop faster than quiet ones | Brakes don't matter downhill
test: Arjun: "I'm basically a lorry driver now." After Level 2, which roads? | Quiet roads, and Level 3 for busier ones | Motorways straight away, he's trained | Only the playground, for ever | Any road, any time
test: "Hills are the worst, eh?" What helps Jack on hills? | Using his gears and pedalling smoothly | Pedalling in the hardest gear for more power | Leaning far back off the saddle | Stopping pedalling to rest on the climb
test: Someone in a river shouts for help. Your mate wants to dive in. Best plan? | Call 999 and throw something that floats | Both of you dive in together to share the weight | Wade in up to your waist and grab them | Go home and say nothing
exam: Identify the four recognised swimming strokes. | Front crawl, backstroke, breaststroke and butterfly | Front crawl, sidestroke, sculling and diving | Butterfly, treading, floating and paddling | Backstroke, diving, gliding and breaststroke
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the external appearance of a person who is drowning. | The person is frequently silent and unable to wave | The person invariably screams and splashes | The person always calls for assistance by name | The person swims vigorously towards the bank
exam: Pursuant to the advice of the RNLI, ascertain the recommended action upon an unexpected immersion in cold water. | To lean back and float until breathing is controlled | To swim with maximum effort towards the shore | To remove all clothing immediately in the water | To submerge the head repeatedly to adjust to the cold
exam: Whereas a pet has fallen into a canal, determine the conduct appropriate for a child upon the bank. | To summon help and to reach or throw from the bank | To enter the water and retrieve the animal | To wade into the canal up to the chest | To leap into the water from a height to reach the animal
